"## Passage retrieval and sentence-level evidence extraction methods from Wikipedia for claim verification: TF-IDF/BM25, dense retrieval, neural ranking, evidence aggregation pipelines (GEAR, GEAR^bar, HAN, KGAT)\n# Passage Retrieval & Evidence Extraction for Wikipedia-Based Claim Verification\n\n## 1. Task setup (FEVER)\n\nThe standard benchmark is **FEVER** [1]: ~185k claims (≈145k train / ≈20k labeled dev / held-out blind test) annotated against a 2016 Wikipedia dump (~5.4M articles), with labels **SUPPORTS / REFUTES / NOT ENOUGH INFO (NEI)**. Gold evidence is 1–3 sentences, possibly spread over multiple pages; NEI claims have none. Systems are 3-stage pipelines: **(1) document retrieval → (2) sentence-level evidence extraction → (3) label aggregation**. Metrics: label accuracy, evidence precision/recall/F1 vs. gold evidence sets, and the **FEVER score** (accuracy counted only when returned evidence passes a minimal precision/recall threshold, 0.05 in the original evaluation) [1].\n\n## 2. Stage 1 — Document retrieval\n\n- **Lexical (TF-IDF/BM25):** the FEVER baseline retrieves pages with Elasticsearch-style term matching (TF-IDF/BM25) [1]. FEVER claims are entity-centric (usually containing the page title), so lexical retrieval is a strong baseline.\n- **Dense retrieval:** later work uses BERT/DPR-style bi-encoders fine-tuned on FEVER evidence with hard negatives, typically combined with BM25 (hybrid) plus cross-encoder re-ranking [7]. Notably, dense retrievers trained on QA-style data **underperform BM25 on FEVER-like entity-centric claims** unless trained in-domain [8]. KILT bundles FEVER with a shared retrieval setting [9].\n- Retrieval quality is the main bottleneck for final FEVER score; GEAR/GEAR^bar/HAN/KGAT all keep the baseline lexical retrieval, so their gains isolate aggregation quality.\n\n## 3. Stage 2 — Sentence-level evidence extraction\n\n- **Baseline [1]:** rank sentences of retrieved pages by TF-IDF cosine similarity with the claim; keep top-k (≈5).\n- **Neural ranking:** pointwise cross-encoder (BERT) scoring of claim–sentence pairs, or pairwise/listwise ranking losses; selection by top-k or threshold.\n- **Entailment-based selection:** UKP-Athene [5] fine-tunes ESIM for 3-way claim–sentence entailment and keeps sentences above an entailment-probability threshold — the strongest 2019-era selector.\n- **Joint extraction+verification:** HAN/MAHN [3] interleaves multi-hop attention between claim and candidate sentences, selecting evidence while verifying.\n\n## 4. Stage 3 — Evidence aggregation / claim verification\n\nMotivation: claims often need multiple sentences across documents; independent per-sentence classification misses cross-evidence interactions.\n\n- **FEVER baseline [1]:** simple feed-forward neural aggregator over claim–sentence representations.\n- **GEAR** (Graph-based Evidence Aggregating and Reasoning) [2]: builds a **fully-connected graph over the k retrieved evidence sentences**; a single round of graph attention propagates information between sentences; each sentence is classified S/R/N; the final verdict combines sentence-level probabilities. Dev accuracy **66.7%**.\n- **GEAR^bar** [2]: the strengthened variant from the same paper — it replaces the simple summation of sentence probabilities with a learned aggregation step over the per-sentence verdicts (the aggregator design KGAT also adopts) [2,4]. Dev **67.6%**.\n- **HAN** (Nie et al. 2019; the multi-hop attention network, MAHN) [3]: hierarchical/multi-hop attention that jointly performs fact extraction and claim verification, iteratively accumulating relevant evidence before the final decision. Dev ≈67%.\n- **KGAT** (Kernel Graph Attention Network) [4]: a **word-level graph** over claim + evidence (BERT-based word representations), finer-grained than GEAR's sentence nodes. Its key idea is **kernel attention**: attention weights are computed by passing pairwise cosine similarities through multiple Gaussian kernels with different means/variances, yielding multi-head attention over different similarity bands — unlike softmax attention, which concentrates only on the most similar tokens. Sentence embeddings are pooled from word nodes, classified per sentence, and combined by a lightweight **logistic-regression aggregator**. Dev **69.8%**, blind test **70.6%**.\n- **UKP-Athene** [5,6]: aggregates ESIM sentence-level entailment/contradiction probabilities (with NEI fallback); **FEVER 2019 shared-task winner, blind test 74.5%** [6].\n\n## 5. Results snapshot (FEVER label accuracy)\n\n| System | Retrieval / selection | Aggregation | Dev | Blind test |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline [1] | ES TF-IDF/BM25 + TF-IDF top-5 sentences | NN aggregator | ≈55 | ≈51 |\n| GEAR [2] | baseline lexical | graph attention + probability sum | 66.7 | – |\n| GEAR^bar [2] | baseline lexical | learned aggregation | 67.6 | – |\n| HAN [3] | joint multi-hop attention | hierarchical aggregation | ≈67 | – |\n| KGAT [4] | baseline lexical | kernel graph attention + LR | 69.8 | 70.6 |\n| UKP-Athene [5,6] | ES + entailment selection | ESIM probability aggregation | ≈71.5 | 74.5 |\n\n## 6. Takeaways\n\n- Aggregation/reasoning over evidence (GEAR → KGAT) yields roughly +11–14 points over the baseline under identical lexical retrieval; better sentence selection (UKP-Athene) adds several more.\n- NEI handling is implicit in aggregation: systems must abstain when no supporting/refuting evidence is retrieved (thresholds or absence-based rules).\n- Later work moved to semantic-level graphs [10], BERT-era end-to-end pipelines (blind test ≈75–76%), and KILT-style unified retrieval+verification [9]; current systems use dense retrieval + cross-encoder re-ranking or LLM-based retrieval-augmented verification.\n\n*Caveat: figures are reported from the papers as I recall them; the precise GEAR^bar formulation is worth verifying directly in [2].*\n\n## References\n\n[1] Thorne et al., \"FEVER: A Large-Scale Dataset for Fact Extraction and VERification,\" NAACL 2018.\n[2] Zhou, Han, Yang, Liu, Wang, Li, \"GEAR: Graph-based Evidence Aggregating and Reasoning for Fact Verification,\" EMNLP-IJCNLP 2019.\n[3] Nie, Feng, Zhao, \"Combining Fact Extraction and Claim Verification with Multi-hop Attention Networks,\" 2019.\n[4] Liu et al., \"Evidence-aware Fact Verification with Kernel Graph Attention Network\" (KGAT), ACL 2020.\n[5] Hanselowski et al., \"UKP-Athene: Multi-Sentence Textual Entailment for Claim Verification,\" ACL 2019.\n[6] Thorne et al., \"The FEVER 2019 Shared Task,\" FEVER Workshop @ EMNLP 2019.\n[7] Karpukhin et al., \"Dense Passage Retrieval for Open-Domain Question Answering,\" EMNLP 2020.\n[8] Sciavolino et al., \"Simple Entity-Centric Questions Challenge Dense Retrievers,\" EMNLP 2021.\n[9] Petroni et al., \"KILT: A Benchmark for Knowledge Intensive Language Tasks,\" NAACL 2021.\n[10] Zhong et al., \"Reasoning over Semantic-Level Graph for Fact Checking,\" ACL 2020.\n\n---\n\n## FEVER 2.0 adversarial claims, AVeriTeC 2024, SciFact, CLEF CheckThat: recent benchmarks and state-of-the-art systems for Wikipedia-based claim verification\nI'll research these fact-checking benchmarks and state-of-the-art systems systematically.\n\n---\n\n## How Wikipedia structure (entity linking, hyperlinks, tables, revision history, citations) is used as evidence for fact-checking and claim support in NLP research\nResearch on 'How Wikipedia structure (entity linking, hyperlinks, tables, revision history, citations) is used as evidence for fact-checking and claim support in NLP research' failed: Connection error."